// expect-exit 2
//
// A list has one element type, and pushing a second kind into it is a type
// error rather than something to discover later.
//
// Before the element type existed there was nowhere to put this. `var mixed = []
// mixed.push(1) mixed.push("two")` stored an int and a string in the same
// 8-byte slot, in whatever order they arrived, and every read of it was a
// `*(void**)`. Nothing said so at the push and nothing could say so at the
// read, because the read had no type to check the value against. That is the
// mechanism behind F-0009, where `getOr(nums, 0, "d")` on an int list
// dereferenced an int as a `char*` and the process died with SIGSEGV.
//
//     $ orbit run tests/suite/list_mixed_element_kinds.orb
//     orbit: list element type mismatch
//       list holds int, this value is string
//       a list has one element type, so a read and a write must agree on it
//
// Exit 2 is what orbit_list_kind_error exits with.
//
// A heterogeneous list is not expressible in this shape of list. The
// alternative is a boxed element -- a tagged union per slot -- which is a much
// larger runtime change and still would not make `getOr(l, 0, "d")` legal,
// because the read would have no declared type to check against either.
fn main() -> int {
    var mixed = []
    mixed.push(1)
    print("first push accepted")
    mixed.push("two")
    print("unreachable")
    return 0
}
